  "summary": "Aesthetic philosophy emphasizing rustic simplicity and imperfection in tea ceremony",
  "description": "Characterized by handmade, irregular tea bowls and natural materials reflecting transience and humility; emerged from Sen no Rikyū's rejection of ostentation in favor of profound simplicity.",
  "culturalContext": "By the latter years of the Muromachi period, tea ceremony had become widespread, with a preference for expensive wares of Chinese origin known as karamono.  Wabi-cha evolved as part of a movement to appreciate local wares and simpler styles.\n\nGenerally, three main figures are credited with the development of the wabi-cha aesthetic form of chanoyu: first, Murata Jukō; then, Takeno Jōō; and finally, Sen no Rikyū.\n\nRikyū cited two poems from the Shin Kokin Wakashū poetry anthology of the early thirteenth century, as exemplifying his wabi aesthetic. One, a favorite of Takeno Jōō's, is by Fujiwara no Teika (1162–1241):\n\nThe other, in which Rikyū found particular appeal, is by Fujiwara Ietaka (1158–1237):",
